    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, it absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats again and again. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, slowing down bacteria so your food lasts longer."
